<h2>Indian cumin seed for export</h2>
<p>India's second-largest spice export by value moves through Gujarat and Rajasthan, with Unjha — Asia's largest cumin trading market — as the central hub for grading, cleaning and export processing. We supply machine-clean and sortex-clean cumin, singles or splits, matched to your purity requirement.</p>

<p>Harvest runs February-April. Well-dried cumin stores for many months without significant quality loss when kept cool and out of direct light, which supports year-round supply from stock rather than a narrow seasonal window.</p>

<h2>How should I specify cumin?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cleaning grade.</strong> Machine-clean (98%+ purity) suits most bulk food-manufacturing orders. Sortex-clean (99.5%+) uses optical sorting for buyers who need tighter quality control or are packing for retail.</li>
  <li><strong>Singles vs. splits.</strong> Whole (single) seeds are the standard commercial form; splits can be relevant for certain processing uses — tell us which you need.</li>
  <li><strong>Volatile oil content.</strong> The source of cumin's aroma and flavour strength, typically 2.5-4.0% and season-dependent — confirmed on the certificate of analysis for your specific lot.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which markets buy Indian cumin?</h2>
<p>China is the largest single destination by value, with Bangladesh, the UAE, the United States and the UK following — see the cited country breakdown below.</p>
<p>Shipping specifically to <a href="/products/spices/cumin/china/">China</a>, <a href="/products/spices/cumin/bangladesh/">Bangladesh</a> or <a href="/products/spices/cumin/uae/">the UAE</a>? Each has a dedicated route page with the registration, grade and logistics detail for that market.</p>

<h2>Cumin export pricing</h2>
<p>Cumin pricing is sensitive to Gujarat/Rajasthan crop conditions and moves more than most commodity spices year to year. Send your required cleaning grade, quantity and destination port and we'll quote current terms.</p>

